\begin{center}
{\Large Tasks and Schedulers in Real-Time Systems: Part 1}
\end{center}

<*>
%%%%** Section 1 
\section{Tasks, Processes, and Threads}
%%%%** Section 1.1
\subsection{Processor State / Context}

The CPU of a computer has several state variables.  Once they are
specified we know the exact state of the CPU.  These include:
\begin{itemize}
        \item Program Counter (location in memory of next instruction)
        \item Value of each CPU register
        \item Processor Status Flags
        \item Stack Pointer
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2
\subsection{Tasks or Processes}

A {\it Task} or {\it Process} is a unit of code and data which is
described by one processor state\footnote{AKA ``process context"}  when it is running. 

Example Tasks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Flash an LED for 100ms, repeat every 1.5 seconds.
    \item Send a byte of data over a communication link whenever a hardware bit indicates
    the com. link is ready.
    \item Every 60 seconds, read an analog voltage from a Thermistor, convert it to a resistance, calibrate to temperature. 
\end{itemize}

A task is usually implemented in {\tt C} by writing a function.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsection{Context Switching:}

A computer must often switch from one task to another (don't we all?) and then back.

To restart a suspended task, we have to be able to {\it restore} its state. 

To restore its state, we have to save it.  

Thus a context switch means
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Save context of the processor for the current task.
  \item Restore previously stored context of new task.
  \item Start the new task.
\end{enumerate}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsection{Types of Tasks:}

%%%%** Section 1.2.1 
\subsubsection{Periodic Tasks}\label{Periodic}
\begin{itemize}
        \item Found in  Hard-Real time applications
        \item Examples: 1) Control: Every 10 ms., Read sensors $\to$ compute control $\to$ output
command\\
          2) Multimedia: Every 22.727$\mu$sec,  Get music sample $\to$ compute DSP filter $\to$ output
sample to DAC.

        \item Characterized by three attributes:
                \begin{enumerate}
                        \item $P$, Period: the regular time interval between runs of this task.
                        \item $C$, Computing Resources: How much CPU time  does it require
each time. Obviously $C <= P$. ($C$ may not be the same each time.
<*chn>
Different code branches in the task may take different amounts of time. Use $C=C_{max}$.
<*>
                        \item $D$, Deadline: How quickly must the task me completed after it
is started each time tick. $C < D < P$
                \end{enumerate}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   compute for C seconds;
   return();
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2.2 
\subsubsection{Intermittent Tasks}
\begin{itemize}
        \item Found in  all types of applications
        \item Examples: 1) Send an email every night at 4:00 AM\\
        2) Save all data when power is going down \\
        3) Send a message to plant operator when tank runs low.\\
        4) Calibrate a sensor on startup.

        \item Characterized by two attributes:
                \begin{enumerate}
                        \item $C$, Computing Resources: How much CPU time  does it require each time.
                        \item $D$, Deadline: How quickly must the task be completed after it
is started. (whenever that happens to be).
                \end{enumerate}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   compute for C seconds;
   if(! done) return();
   else halt_me();
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2.3 
\subsubsection{Background Tasks}
\begin{itemize}
        \item A soft real time or non real time task.
        \item Lower Priority
        \item Will be accomplished only as CPU time is available when no
        hard real time tasks are ready.
        \item Characterized by:
                \begin{itemize}
                         \item $C$, Computing Resources: How much CPU time  does
                         it require each time between scheduler accesses.
                \end{itemize}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   compute for C seconds;
   if(! done) return();
   else halt_me();
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.2.4 
\subsubsection{Complex Tasks}
\begin{itemize}
        \item Found in all types of applications
        \item Examples: 1) Microsoft Word \hspace{0.15in}     2) Apache Web Server.

        \item Characteristics:
                \begin{enumerate}
                        \item Continuous need for CPU time.
                        \item Frequent requests for I/O which free up the CPU.
                        \item Waits for user input which free up CPU.
                \end{enumerate}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   while(1) {
      compute for C seconds;
      request_IO();  // also starts scheduler
      }
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}
<*chn>
I/O activity frees up the CPU because the task must wait, usually a significant amount of
time, for the I/O activity on a physical device.  It cannot compute between the time it requests
the I/O and the time it gets the data or writes the data so now the CPU is free for other tasks.
<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.3
\subsection{Task States}\label{TaskStates}
Tasks are in one of four states:
\begin{enumerate}
        \item {\tt Running} 
        \item {\tt Ready} to Run (but not running)
        \item {\tt Waiting}    (for something {\it other} than the CPU.)
        \item {\tt Inactive}   
\end{enumerate}
<*chn>
Only one task can be {\bf Running} at a time (unless we are using a ``multicore" CPU).  
A task which is waiting for the CPU 
is {\bf Ready}.  When a task has requested
I/O or put itself to sleep, it is {\tt Waiting}.
An {\tt Inactive} task is waiting to be allowed into the schedule.  It is like 
Microsoft Word when you are NOT running it. 

We'll see later how a task can get into or out of the {\tt Inactive} state.

<*>
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}




%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%55
%
%%%%** Section 2 
\section{Threads: Smaller Units of Computation}
We have talked about:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Programs
	\item Tasks
	\item Subroutines/Functions\footnote{When I use a slash (/) in a list like this I mean that the two terms are equivalent}
	\item Processes
	\item Threads
\end{itemize}
Confused yet??


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
With any complex computing system we naturally break it up into {\it units }.

For example in the EE474 lab exercise:  ``tasks"
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Each unit has

\begin{itemize}
	\item Code/instructions
	\item Data
	\item Context/State
	\item Resources (memory, I/O devices, semaphores)
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*chn>
\begin{center}
%\vspace{2.5in}
   \includegraphics[width=3in]{sched_figs/00264.eps}
\end{center}

<s>\scalebox{1.0}{
<s>\includegraphics{sched_figs/00264.eps} }


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

A {\it Program or Process} is a unit of computation with all of the above attributes.

A {\it Subroutine or Function} is a unit of computation with code, data, and context, but
no managed resources.

<*hn> I am using the term ``managed resources" to mean resources which can be assigned and de-assigned
by the operating system such as memory or I/O devices.   Of course a function within a program has its
own memory, but if it requests memory from the OS (for example with {\tt malloc()}, the OS assigns it
to the program, not to the function within that program.

<*>

A {\it Thread} is a unit of computation with code and context, but no private data.  Threads may even
share code with each other.

Threads are owned by a program/process.

Threads are like pieces within a program which can be scheduled by the OS independently.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*chn>
\begin{center}
%\vspace{2.5in}
   \includegraphics[width=3in]{sched_figs/00265.eps}
\end{center}
<*>
<s>  \scalebox{1.0}{  \includegraphics{sched_figs/00265.eps} }

A complete software system with two processes.   
<s>Process 1: normal, Process 2: threaded
<chn>Process 1 is a ``normal" single-threaded process.
<chn>Process 2 is a multithreaded process.

<chn>Scheduler manages 6 units:  Process 1 and the 5 threads of process 2.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2.1
\subsection{Thread Context Switching}
Since threads have a smaller context than programs, context switching is faster.



\begin{itemize}
	\item Only save/restore CPU state
	\item No need to change memory setup

<chn>(with programs in systems bigger than the 16-bit 80188, this can be elaborate).

\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Why use threads?

An example.

Web server.
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Web server pseudocode:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1)  {
    OS_read_network();      // get incoming request
    compute;                // figure out what html page to get
    OS_Disk_I/O();          // read disk file for www page
    OS_write_network();	    // send page to user
}
\end{verbatim}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Alternative 1:  Run above as a single process.

Problem:  When process is waiting for network, disk is idle and vice versa.
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Alternative 2:   Set up several threads of same code:

Advantage:   Scheduler can start a thread which is done with disk I/O while
another thread is waiting on network and vice versa.

CPU is used more efficiently and system delivers more pages per second with same hardware performance.

Potential Problem:  more time spent context switching.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2.2
\subsection{Types of threads.}

There are lots of possibilities for size of {\it units}.

The more context a thread contains, the ``heavier" it is.

Heavier threads take more time for context switching.

OS designer determines what kind of threads to support --- application programmer gets only one
option.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 3 
\section{RTOS Scheduling Concepts}
<hcn>RTOS = Real Time Operating System

%%%%** Section 3.1
\subsection{Time}\label{Time}


Time is measured by a piece of hardware which records actual
clock time: a {\it real time clock}:
<chn>We use the terms

``Time Quanta'' ``Time Slice''  ``Ticks''

Let's call the period of time ticks: $T$.

Typically $T < P_{min}$. Sometimes $T << P_{min}$.

<*chn>
Where $P_{min}$ is the shortest period of all tasks in the  system.
In other words, the operating system measures time in units, $T$, which
are smaller than the shortest period of any task.
<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.2
\subsection{Soft vs. Hard Real Time}

{\bf Real Time:}  A software system with specific speed or response
time requirements.

{\bf Soft Real Time:} If the deadlines are not met,
performance is considered low.

{\bf Hard Real Time:} A computer system in which at least one task must meet
deadlines in time.  If the deadlines are not met, the system has failed.

{\bf Super Hard Real Time:} Mostly Periodic Tasks:
Task periods = the OS time tick, task compute times ($C$) and
deadlines ($D$) are very
short. $P = D = T, C << P$.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.3
\subsection{Scheduling}

At each opportunity, OS asks:
If $N$ tasks are {\bf Ready}, which one should I run?

If you can show that scheduler always can achieve deadlines, 
the system is ``deterministically schedulable".


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.4
\subsection{Scheduling Goals}
\begin{enumerate}
\item CPU Utilization:
\[
U_{CPU} = 1 - \frac{idle}{period}
\]
Tradional measure for mainframes.  100\% is better.

{\bf But} $U_{CPU} ~= 100\%$ is not safe for real-time systems

Goal:   low load - 40\%, high load - 90\%.

\item Throughput: 
Work units completed per unit time.

\item Turnaround Time:
Total time from task start to completion (waiting + execution + I/O)

\item Waiting Time: 
Choose scheduler which minimizes waiting time. 

\item Response Time **
How quickly system responds to external asynchronus events. 
\end{enumerate}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\section*{Scheduler Types:}
%%%%** Section 3.4.1 
\subsection{Periodic Schedulers}
<chn>\begin{itemize}

<chn>        \item
Infinite Loop
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Most primitive of all
                \item Also known as Non-preemptive Round Robin.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
while(1)
{
task1_fcn();
task2_fcn();
task3_fcn();
...
}
                \end{verbatim}
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly
                \item $C << P$, $P=T$.

\begin{verbatim}
taskN_fcn()
{
compute a little bit;
return();
}
\end{verbatim}
      \end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>        \item
Synchronized Infinite Loop
        \begin{itemize}
                \item the top of the loop waits on a hardware clock.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
while(1)
{
wait(CLOCK_PULSE);
task1_fcn();
task2_fcn();
task3_fcn();
...
}
                \end{verbatim}
                \item Each task {\bf must} voluntarily return to
                scheduler quickly, as above.
                \item $C << P$, $T=P$.
        \end{itemize}

<chn>\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsection{Pre-emption}
Each function in these schedulers must ``voluntarily" return back to the main loop, 
and must make sure that it doesn't compute for too much time.   If a task gets
stuck, it breaks the whole system.  

The fix for this is {\bf preemption} - the ability to break into a running task and 
stop it. In other words to force a context switch. 


<*chn>
The schedulers above are appropriate when all tasks are periodic and hard real-time.
However, because execution times ($C$) vary, we have to allow  extra time in
each loop of the non-preemptive round robin schedule (taken up by the ``wait()'' function).

<*>

<s>\end{slide}
